course: dictionary
lesson: 12814
title: Emancipation: being set free
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Emancipation is a noun. It means being set free from slavery, or from strict control by other people. It can also mean getting equal rights and freedoms, for example for women or for a group of people.
explain: «The emancipation of the enslaved people changed their lives forever.» The clues are changed their lives and forever. Before, people were owned by others. After emancipation, they were free. Ask yourself what word would fit: freedom fits well here.
explain: «Women's emancipation brought them the vote and equal rights.» This sentence is about rights, not chains. Use the swap check: put «freedom» in place of «emancipation». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: «Emancipation» means being set free from slavery or from strict control by others.
fact: «Emancipation» can also mean getting equal rights and freedoms, as in «women's emancipation».
fact: The Emancipation Proclamation was declared by Abraham Lincoln in 1863. It declared enslaved people free in the Confederate states.
fact: Slavery was abolished in the British Empire between 1833 and 1834.
fact: Emancipation is an uncountable noun, so we say «the emancipation», not «an emancipation».
exercise: «The emancipation of the enslaved people was a huge change in history.» What does emancipation mean here? | Learning to read and write | *Being set free from slavery | Moving to a new town | Being sold to a new owner
exercise: «Women's emancipation brought them the vote and equal rights.» Which meaning is closest? | Losing all their rights | Going on a long holiday | Being given a new house | *Getting equal rights and freedoms
exercise: Which event is linked to the year 1863 and Abraham Lincoln? | Women winning the vote | The building of a castle | The abolition of slavery in the British Empire | *The Emancipation Proclamation
test: Is emancipation a countable or an uncountable noun? | Countable | Plural | Verb | *Uncountable
test: Which sentence uses emancipation correctly? | *The emancipation of the slaves was a major step in history. | The emancipation was a very tall tree. | She emancipation her bike every day. | I had an emancipation for lunch.
exam: Who declared the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863? | Queen Victoria | Winston Churchill | *Abraham Lincoln | Napoleon Bonaparte
